Pretty much most of the people.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:01.390
Awesome.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.220
So let's see how it works.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:04.660
When you deploy a
new application,

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.260
you would probably
start with one machine,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.280
deploy application to this
machine, test it, run it,

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.190
and you might have a few users.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:15.910
This setup is quite
easy to manage manually.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:17.500
Or maybe not that easy.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.200
How do you know what
machine type to pick?

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.300
How much CPU or
memory do you need?

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.640
You probably could take
the same machine size

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.760
as you use in your on-prem
data center before,

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.500
but Compute Engine
allows you fine,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.200
granular configuration
of how many CPU cores

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.797
or how much memory you can pick.

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:40.130
There is a good chance that
there is a machine type that

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.440
is just as well fit
for your workload,

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.490
but is smaller and cheaper.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.130
How do you know that
you don't waste money?

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:52.140
And once your workload grows
and you get more users,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.990
you will need multiple
machines to run it.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.240
This is where all kinds
of problems might start,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.500
and it is no longer feasible to
manage this type of Setup menu.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.110
Let's look at what kind of
problems those could be.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.289
First of all, your application
might stop responding or crash.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:10.680
Some of the users might
experience interruptions

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.280
or latencies in your service.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.670
Another problem is in the rare
event a whole zone might fail,

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.750
and if you have deployed all
your machines in a single zone,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.060
your customers might
experience complete outage.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.110
This is not something that
you want to experience.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.450
Or your load might
spike and land

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.620
an insufficient number of
VMs, and the extra customers

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.740
that you have again might
experience longer latency

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.180
or not be served at all.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.530
Something you don't
want to happen.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.010
Or maybe you have provisioned
enough of the machines

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:47.590
to serve even your
highest peaks, but then

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.340
what when the weekend comes, for
example, and your usage drops?

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.370
You don't want to overpay for
all those extra machines that

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.900
are sitting idle.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.760
And on top of all of
that, you will probably

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.190
continuously improve
your application

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:02.590
and run all kinds of updates.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:05.740
Security patches, new features.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:09.160
How do you roll out the updates
safely and avoid the disruption

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.110
in case of a bad
update for users?

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.210
When any of these
problems happen,

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.280
you don't want to
get paged, especially

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.270
in the middle of the night.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:22.780
Because when coming to Cloud,
you expect your infrastructure

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.360
to run flawlessly.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.760
And the good news is that
managed instance groups

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.200
and Compute Engine allows you
to take care of these problems

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.480
automatically and build
you highly available,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.660
scalable applications where
you can roll out updates

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.040
automatically and
safely, and also pick

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.460
the optimal size of the
machines for your workloads.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.040
So that you can
have a peace of mind

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.370
knowing that your
application is taken care of.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.850
In today's session, we will look
how to deploy an application

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:57.620
to a managed instance group.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:01.100
We'll build a stateless
load-balanced application.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.640
And we will also look into
how to apply VM rightsizing

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.782
recommendations
to those machines.

00:04:06.782 --> 00:04:09.240
And if you're already familiar
with some of these concepts,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.880
we'll have our pro tips for you.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.350
So let's start with the
application deployment.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.130
You likely have some
kind of CI/CD pipeline

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:22.019
which results in an artifact.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.870
You could package your
application in container image

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.210
or perhaps prebake
it together with all

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.700
of the dependencies on
the boot disk image,

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.740
or maybe write a
startup script on top

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.350
of a plain vanilla
operating system

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.810
so that it loads and boots
your application on VM start.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.750
Any of these approaches
work with what we're

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.590
going to talk about today.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:46.350
But for the sake of example,
let's pick container image.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:50.430
Deploying container image
is easy on Compute Engine

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.200
and takes only two steps.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.070
You create an instance, just as
you would in your regular VM.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.950
You click the checkbox that
you want to deploy container.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.170
Specify which container to run,
and some of the parameters.

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:07.800
Let's say, restart policy, or
you could pass some environment

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.720
variables to your container.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.510
And what happens is
that our Compute Engine

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.550
will create virtual
machine based

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.000
on the container optimized
operating system image, which

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.260
already has Docker and container
startup agent pre-installed.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.780
And it will boot your container
on VM start automatically.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.990
You don't need to write
a new startup script.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.300
And on top of that, whenever
you create a new machine

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.700
you could use the latest
version of the container image

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.610
with all of the updates
and security patches

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:39.050
that are managed by Google.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:41.730
So if one machine
is not enough, you

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:45.220
can also deploy your
application to multiple machines

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:46.550
in a managed instance group.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:50.080
So the way you do it is that
you create instance template

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.220
in exactly the same way as
it's for a virtual machine.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.220
And then instance
template is like a recipe

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.160
for creating a machine that
managed instance groups uses

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.240
to create your VMs.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.170
And if your application
is load balanced,

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:08.830
you can add your instance
group as a back-end service

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.240
to a load balancer.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:11.720
That's it.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.240
So let's see how it works.

