Thank you.

00:08:57.048 --> 00:08:58.090
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: Yeah.

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:00.460
So we have deployed a
simple stateless application

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.500
to an instance group.

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:07.480
But now how do we make it
highly available and scalable?

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:11.230
But let's first talk about what
a stateless application is.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:14.080
A good example of it would
be, for example, the front end

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:15.490
of a web store.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:17.260
You want it to be
highly available

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:20.590
so that your customers don't
get HTP-500 during checkout

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.750
process, and you want
it to be scalable

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:24.700
so that you can get all
the business you can,

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:29.550
even during the spikes,
such as on Black Friday.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:33.150
And stateless VMs are
like a herd of sheep.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.360
You care only about
how many you have.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:38.580
You can easily replace
one with another.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.710
And the machines work
exactly the same way

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:43.840
and they don't care
any state on them.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.250
So let's go back to the
problems that might happen.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:52.710
What if application stops
responding or crashes?

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:54.690
I see you have a question there.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:55.980
Could you put it on Dory?

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.710
And then we'll
address it in the end.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:08.090
So if the application stops
responding or crashes,

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.850
autohealing will help
to take care of that.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:14.150
It will automatically
recreate the instances.

00:10:16.478 --> 00:10:18.020
It will detect that
they have failed,

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:19.880
and automatically
recreate them to keep them

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:25.510
in service so that your
customers can be served again.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:27.140
Configuring autohealing is easy.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:29.200
All you need to do is
to create a health check

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.390
and attach it to the
managed instance group.

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.280
And the way the
health checking works

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.080
is that it pings
your application

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:37.400
on a regular basis in the
specified port and protocol,

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:41.170
let's say HTTP 80, on
the paths that you show,

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:44.800
and it will expect
HTTP 200 response

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.370
that everything is OK
within a predefined latency

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:49.930
that you can configure.

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:53.010
And if the health check fails
multiple times in a row--

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.300
and again, you can configure
how many times this is,

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.490
let's say two failures--
then the instance

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:02.110
is deemed unhealthy and
autohealing will kick in

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:04.690
to recreate it from the instance
template with the well test

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:06.160
configuration.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:09.300
And health checking
works for both HTTP,

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:14.270
HTTPS protocols, as
well as TCP and SSL.

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:15.890
What's important
to note is that you

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:18.380
can create exactly
the same health checks

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:20.150
for load balancing purposes.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:23.000
And load balancer users
health checks differently

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.190
than managed instance groups.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:27.320
When the VM is
marked as unhealthy,

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:30.480
load balancer does not send
new traffic to the machine,

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.390
and will send it to
the other machines that

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:33.590
are still healthy.

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:35.540
At the same time,
managed instance group

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:38.000
will recreate a machine that
isn't healthy to bring it back

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:38.510
in service.

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:40.460
These are very
different actions.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:43.310
And for that reason,
we highly recommend

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:45.710
you to create different health
checks for a load balancer

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:48.860
and for managed instance group,
and make health checks more

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:50.540
aggressive for a load balancer.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:53.630
More aggressive meaning
shorter check intervals,

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:56.360
expect lower response
latency, and maybe

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:58.400
a lower number of
failed health checks

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:00.500
to actually mark it unhealthy.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:03.680
This is important because
imagine a situation when you

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:06.150
have a machine that is
serving at its full capacity,

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:10.040
so it cannot really receive
more traffic and will produce

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:11.030
latencies.

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:14.030
You want the load balancer
to redirect this traffic

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:15.350
to other machines.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:17.990
At the same time, the
machine is perfectly

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:20.030
healthy, it's serving
existing connections,

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:24.480
so you don't want the autohealer
to kick in and recreate it.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:28.790
So to make sure that
in such situation

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:30.980
your machine is
not recreated, you

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:34.940
should give autohealer a little
bit more time for the machine

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:37.580
to recover and
start serving again.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:40.280
Or if it's really unhealthy
for a longer period,

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:42.130
only then recreate it.

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:45.680
That's why you need
two health checks.

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:48.810
Another pro tip is that, when
you configure autohealing,

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:52.950
make sure you set initial
delay to a higher value

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:56.040
than it takes for
your machine to start.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.230
During the initial
delay, autohealing

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:00.930
will not touch
unhealthy machines

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:03.330
to give enough time for
the application to boot.

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:04.830
Of course, while
VM is booting, it

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:08.230
will be unhealthy because your
application is not yet serving.

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:12.420
So if you set initial delay to
longer than it takes to serve,

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:14.230
for the application
to start serving,

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:16.770
you will make sure that
autohealing will only

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.440
recreate a machine when
it is really unhealthy

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.300
and not because
it's still booting.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:24.070
So let's see how all
autohealing works.

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:30.100
CEZARY PIEKACZ: All right.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:34.470
So we have an instance group
with three VMs up and running.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:37.410
All of the VMs are running
the same web application.

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:38.990
This is a simple Python script.

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:40.870
It has a few features.

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:43.410
One of the features is
reporting the health status

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:44.230
of an application.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:49.770
So if we click here, we can
verify that our application

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:50.870
is up and running.

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:52.990
It's serving HTTP 200 response.

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:54.990
So it's basically saying
to the managed instance

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:55.698
group, that, yes.

00:13:55.698 --> 00:13:56.260
I'm OK.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:59.090
I can accept traffic,
and I can serve traffic.

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:03.250
So what I did is I created
a simple method that

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:06.030
will just start reporting
unhealthy status.

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:09.870
So what it means is that
once I open the same URL,

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:11.410
it's basically
saying, I'm sorry.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:12.252
I've crashed.

00:14:12.252 --> 00:14:13.210
I will not accept any--

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:15.460
I should not accept any
connections right now.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:17.260
So it's telling
managed instance group

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.968
that this VM is not responding.

00:14:19.968 --> 00:14:21.260
The application is not running.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:23.380
So it should be recreated.

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:24.580
Let's go here.

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:28.360
It usually takes a minute or
two for the managed instance

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:30.190
group to notice it.

00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:34.870
So let's go and look how the
health check is configured.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:37.000
So we've set up the
health check, autohealing.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:38.375
So let's go to
the health checks.

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:42.540
Autohealing.

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:46.100
So we can see that it's
looking at the path /health.

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:48.530
So it's the same that
I've used previously.

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:51.560
It's using the HTTP
protocol on port 80.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:54.960
It's pinging that application
every five seconds,

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.830
and it's waiting to for
two consecutive failures

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:01.190
to notice that it's unhealthy.

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:03.080
Let's go back to our
managed instance group.

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:09.430
Autohealing.

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:13.180
And you can see that managed
instance group detected

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:15.760
that an application crashed
and that the VM should

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:18.280
be recreated, and it's
already recreating.

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:20.830
So after a minute it
will be up and running,

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:23.950
and your VMs will
accept traffic,

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:26.675
and everything will be fine.

00:15:26.675 --> 00:15:29.430
Can you switch back
to slides, please?

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:30.680
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: All right.

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:34.220
So we have just
protected our application

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.930
against failure on
a single machine.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.890
But what if there is
a more rare event when

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:42.260
there is a misconfiguration
or something happens

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:45.273
and the whole zone is out?

00:15:45.273 --> 00:15:46.940
Autohealing will not
help you with that,

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:49.340
because even if it tries
to recreate the machines,

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:50.780
the problem is not
on the machine.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:53.520
It's on the zonal level.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:58.280
So in order to protect
your workload against that,

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:01.820
you can deploy your
machines to multiple zones

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:06.120
in the region using regional
managed instance groups.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:08.510
And in this example,
the way it works

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:10.850
that even if a
single zone fails,

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:13.610
the load balancer will send
traffic to the other zones

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:15.770
where the machines
are still available,

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:19.250
and your customers
will still be served.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:21.180
It's worth noting
that on this picture,

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.600
though, that you lose one
third of your capacity.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.690
So let's say if you
need nine VMs to serve

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:30.110
all of your traffic, if a
zone fails, you get only six.

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:34.790
So if you want to really
have enough capacity to, even

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:38.030
in case of a zone failure,
to serve all of your users,

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:42.530
you should provision extra VMs
so that 2/3 of the total number

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.220
is enough to serve the traffic.

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:47.220
So in this example, for
example, if you deploy,

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:52.160
let's say six extra VMs,
even if a single zone fails,

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:55.070
you'll still have 10 VMs, which
is more than enough to cover

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:58.190
your requirement of 9.

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:02.600
And the way to calculate how
many extra VMs to provision

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:03.420
is easy.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:08.480
You just divide the
number of total VMs

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:10.880
that you need to serve
your workload by the number

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:12.660
of the remaining serving zones.

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:16.040
So for example, if you deploy
your group to three zones,

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:18.859
you need to provision
50% extra machines

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:20.910
so that even in case
of zone failure,

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:24.990
you have enough machines to
serve all of your traffic.

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:28.860
So let's see how this works.

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:29.670
CEZARY PIEKACZ: OK.

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:31.910
So we have another
managed instance group.

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:37.040
This time it's deployed
in three different zones.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:40.730
You can verify it here that it's
deployed in US central-1 b, c,

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:41.270
and f.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:46.120
And in each zone, we have two
instances that are running.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:48.200
I've also configured
a load balancer

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:52.110
that will direct traffic
to all of the instances.

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:53.210
Let's go open it up.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:04.200
And you can see that on each--

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:06.358
refresh should work.

00:18:06.358 --> 00:18:07.650
Just [INAUDIBLE] one more time.

00:18:10.632 --> 00:18:11.630
Yeah.

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:15.120
On each refresh we
get a VM that is

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:17.540
serving from a different zone.

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:21.860
So what I did is I
wrote a simple script.

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:22.360
HTML.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:30.690
That will just scrub the web
page from each of the instance,

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:34.630
get the zone that the
instance is running in,

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:36.330
and it will print
it on the screen.

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.960
So you can see that right
now, we have instances

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:41.740
serving from all of the zones.

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:45.390
So how to do tests on the--

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:46.770
how to test if the zone fails.

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:49.950
So I cannot bring the
whole zone down, obviously,

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:53.850
but what I can do is I can
simulate it by disabling health

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:55.080
checks.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:56.760
Sorry, not disabling, like--

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:01.600
start making our VMs report
unhealthy from particular zone.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:04.020
So let's do that.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:04.910
[INAUDIBLE]

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:14.660
Disable US central-1.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:15.160
Perfect.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:19.690
All right.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:24.950
So what I did, as I told you,
right now, VMs from zone F

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:27.350
are starting to report
that are unhealthy.

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:30.255
So load balancer will notice
it in a matter of seconds

00:19:30.255 --> 00:19:31.630
and will start
directing traffic.

00:19:31.630 --> 00:19:33.490
You can already see
that we are serving

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:36.670
traffic only from zone
B and zone C. So let's

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:51.258
also disable zone B.

00:19:51.258 --> 00:19:52.245
Few more seconds.

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:55.190
All right.

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:57.070
You can see that load balancing.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:57.920
Notice it.

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:00.440
And right now we are
serving only from zone C.

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:04.050
So let's bring
back all the zones.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:04.550
Back up.

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:09.696
That's right.

00:20:09.696 --> 00:20:10.780
US central-1.

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:17.330
All right.

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:21.130
So all the VMs are
reporting healthy.

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:24.740
So load balancer
pings each of the VMs.

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:27.200
Notice that it's starting
to report healthy

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:30.560
and it can accept traffic,
and will start directing

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:32.730
the traffic back to those VMs.

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:35.840
You already see that
zone B is up and running,

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:39.710
and zone F will be available
in a matter of seconds.

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:42.850
Can we get back to
the slides, please?

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:44.100
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: All right.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:47.670
So now we have a highly
available application.

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:50.790
We've protected it against
the application failure

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:53.700
in the single machines, and
also against the more rare event

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:56.190
of a whole zonal failure.

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:59.550
But how about the problems
when a load spikes?

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:02.850
Or when you have
a load going down

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:05.880
and excess VMs that you
don't want to pay for it?

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:08.190
Autoscaling helps you with that.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:13.200
It maintains the group size
tailored to the current load

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:17.330
that your service experiences.

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:20.060
Configuring autoscaling is easy.

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:22.760
You set up the load metric
on which you want to scale.

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:27.290
A very popular one is
CPU usage, for example.

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:28.910
You set the target load.

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:30.560
Let's say at 60%.

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:34.910
You can also specify a minimum
and maximum number of VMs

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:37.520
that autoscaler
should not exceed.

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:40.610
Minimum is useful if you want
to have the minimum capacity

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:42.680
that you always
want to be running,

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:44.660
and maximum is to
essentially cap

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:49.160
your budget against the scale up
if you don't want to overspend.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:53.930
And also you need to
specify a cooldown period.

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:56.210
During the cooldown
period, it starts

00:21:56.210 --> 00:22:00.350
when the VM boots and expires
after the number of seconds

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:02.260
you specify.

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:05.620
Autoscaler ignores the
signal from this machine.

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:09.720
This is because on VM
boot, the signal might not

00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:12.030
reflect the actual
real life load,

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:14.490
as many of the
boot processes can

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:17.650
utilize CPUs more than usual.

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:21.480
So make sure you configure a
cooldown period to a higher

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:25.710
value than the VM takes to boot
so that your Autoscaler takes

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:28.620
only the signal that
represents the actual load,

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:33.570
and hence makes more
accurate scaling decisions.

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:35.520
So let's see how
Autoscaling works.

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:38.240
Let's say you have three
overloaded machines

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:40.610
and a target load of 60%.

00:22:40.610 --> 00:22:44.180
Autoscaler will notice
that the average load

00:22:44.180 --> 00:22:47.000
is way above the target,
and will create two more

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:51.180
machines to bring the average
load back to the target.

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:53.900
Now, if the load
goes down over time,

00:22:53.900 --> 00:22:56.360
Autoscaler will notice again
that the average is now

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:57.920
significantly lower
than the target

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:01.790
and will remove the extra
machines to bring it back

00:23:01.790 --> 00:23:02.900
to the target.

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:06.730
That's how it works
in a nutshell.

00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:09.760
And pro tip is that if
you want to make sure

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:13.420
that your workload
scales up really fast,

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:16.960
you should use a
lower target CPU load.

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:18.850
If we look at the
example here, if you

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:21.280
have three overloaded
machines of, let's say,

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:25.210
80%, 70%, and 90% utilization,
if you set the target

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:28.150
load at 60%, Autoscaler
will scale the group size

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:29.960
to four machines.

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:32.110
And if you set the target
lower to a lower value,

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:35.760
like, say, 40%, you
will get six machines,

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:37.080
which is a faster scale up.

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:42.280
And one more tip.

00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:44.550
If you use Autoscaling with
regional managed instance

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:46.650
groups, the same
approach applies

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:49.680
as for fixed size groups
which are not autoscaled.

00:23:49.680 --> 00:23:53.460
Make sure that you set
maximum number of instances

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:56.610
to a higher value that is
needed to serve your load so

00:23:56.610 --> 00:23:59.200
that, even if a whole
zone failure happens,

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:03.390
Autoscaler has enough room to
scale up in the other zones.

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:05.430
The formula for
calculating how much extra

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:09.450
to set for the maximum is the
same as for the fixed size.

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:13.287
The only difference is that
in the fixed size group,

00:24:13.287 --> 00:24:15.120
you will have these
machines always running,

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:17.310
whereas in Autoscaled group
you don't have to pay for them.

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:18.990
This is only a
theoretical maximum

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:23.010
that you allow your
Autoscaler to reach.

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:25.190
So let's look at
Autoscaling in practice.

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:33.040
CEZARY PIEKACZ: OK.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:38.430
So we have one VM that is
running the same web server

00:24:38.430 --> 00:24:40.150
application.

00:24:40.150 --> 00:24:42.690
So I told you that
this web server

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:44.380
has a few different features.

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:47.590
So one was reporting how
healthy or unhealthy status.

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:51.180
The second one is making
the high load on the CPU.

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:52.800
So what it does
in the background

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:55.470
is just multiplying to
run the numbers in a loop

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:56.860
without any delay.

00:24:56.860 --> 00:25:03.460
So it's basically making the
CPU being utilized in 100%.

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:06.630
Let's look how our
Autoscaling is configured.

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:10.000
So we are looking
at the CPU usage.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:12.930
And we want to have
our managed instance

00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:21.192
group scaled to be below 50% on
average usage of all the VMs.

00:25:21.192 --> 00:25:22.650
Minimal number of
instances is one.

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:25.200
That's why you saw one
instance up and running.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:29.010
And we have maximum number
of instances set to five,

00:25:29.010 --> 00:25:31.050
and cooldown periods
set to 30 seconds, which

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:34.860
means that Autoscaler will not
look at the CPU utilization

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:37.950
for the first 30 seconds.

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:39.820
Our application is quite small.

00:25:39.820 --> 00:25:41.820
It starts quite fast.

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:45.570
But if you have very
high, intensive workloads,

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:48.990
for example, creating
cache or something else,

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:51.750
you can put a
bigger number here.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:52.340
Let's go back.

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:56.700
OK.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.530
You can already see that--

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:02.030
can we get them
back to the screen?

00:26:04.922 --> 00:26:05.890
OK.

00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:08.850
You can already
see that Autoscaler

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:13.510
noticed that this first
VM was over-utilized

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:16.420
and started to boot
up additional VMs.

00:26:16.420 --> 00:26:20.710
If those VMs will be also
utilized in over 50%,

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:24.250
Autoscaler will schedule
another VM creation

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:26.470
and bring it up to number five.

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:26.970
Thank you.

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:30.680
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: All right.

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:34.280
So now we have a highly
available Autoscaled

00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:35.450
application.

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:37.460
But how about updates?

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:41.300
How do you roll out the
updates automatically but also

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:44.960
protect your customers
against bad pushes which

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:45.935
sometimes happens?

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:54.180
Autoupdater allows
you to do just that.

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:56.770
But before we go into
how you configure it,

00:26:56.770 --> 00:26:59.560
let's think about
what an update is.

00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:01.930
In the beginning, you
have an instance group

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:03.130
in an old version.

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:06.280
At the end, you want to
have it in the new version.

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:08.140
And update is a
journey of how you

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:10.330
get from the old
version to a new one,

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:13.060
as it takes multiple steps.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:15.280
And you can
configure the journey

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:17.590
with the following parameters.

00:27:17.590 --> 00:27:22.233
First, you can set up how many
machines you want to update.

00:27:22.233 --> 00:27:23.900
Maybe you want to
update the whole group

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:25.810
if this is an urgent fix.

00:27:25.810 --> 00:27:27.610
Maybe you want to
do a canary update

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:29.032
and update only part of it.

00:27:29.032 --> 00:27:31.240
So let's say you roll out
to half of the group to see

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:34.520
how it works before proceeding.

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:37.550
Second parameter is
how many machines

00:27:37.550 --> 00:27:39.830
do you update at the same
time as the machines need

00:27:39.830 --> 00:27:42.410
to be recreated
from a new instance

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:43.440
template for an update.

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:47.190
So this is your
disruption budget.

00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:50.930
Third parameter is how many
extra VMs in the new version

00:27:50.930 --> 00:27:52.940
to add temporarily
for the update?

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:56.240
This is useful if you don't want
to take any of the old machines

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:59.000
down, but keep them in
service and instead create

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:02.180
new machines and new
versions in the new version.

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:04.340
Wait until they start
serving, and only then take

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:07.660
down the old ones.

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:10.980
Finally, you can specify
the minimum wait time,

00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:14.400
which is after the
machines have been updated,

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:16.350
how long to wait
before proceeding

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:19.980
to the next batch in
the rolling update.

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:23.520
You can also use Recreate or
Restart actions for an update.

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:26.550
Recreate is when you recreated
from the new instance template.

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:29.010
Restart is useful
when you use an agent

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:30.660
inside a machine for
an update and you

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:33.130
need to restart to
actuate that update.

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:37.440
With the same rollout
logic as above.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:40.230
And finally, you can make
an updater proactive, which

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:43.380
means that it will do the
rolling update automatically,

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:45.942
or opportunistic, which
means that it will only

00:28:45.942 --> 00:28:47.650
create new machines
when they are needed.

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:49.320
For example, when scaling up.

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:52.630
And it will only delete the old
machines when also it's needed.

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:54.480
For example, if you
resize the group down.

00:28:57.250 --> 00:29:00.040
And you can have even
more fine-grained

00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:03.100
control over update,
for example, which

00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:06.220
specific machines to update,
when, in which order,

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:09.460
by setting the update
for opportunistic change

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:11.980
in the instance
template in the group

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:14.200
and then just recreate
in the machines

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:15.700
that you want to
update manually.

00:29:15.700 --> 00:29:18.550
And on machine creation, they
will pick up the new instance

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:20.500
template and you will
get the new version.

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:23.590
This way, you can be in
full control of the rollout

00:29:23.590 --> 00:29:28.130
if you don't want an
update to be automatic.

00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:30.830
So let's look at some
of the common scenarios.

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:32.800
Rolling update is the first one.

00:29:32.800 --> 00:29:36.350
So imagine you have a
quick fix that you need

00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:38.095
to roll out to the whole group.

00:29:38.095 --> 00:29:41.120
So you set a new
template to 100%.

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:43.310
However, to stay
on the safe side

00:29:43.310 --> 00:29:45.710
and not to disrupt
the service too much,

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:50.040
you say that the roll out, let's
say, three machines at a time.

00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:51.950
And if the update
is good, updater

00:29:51.950 --> 00:29:55.250
will proceed with the
next three machines.

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:59.150
If it's bad-- bad means
it doesn't pass the health

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:00.035
checks--

00:30:00.035 --> 00:30:01.460
the updater will stop.

00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:03.470
And then you can
come and check out

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:07.250
what's happening in the back or
maybe make a rollback decision.

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:09.710
But if everything is
going well, the updater

00:30:09.710 --> 00:30:12.200
will take three machines
by three machines

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:14.300
until the whole
group is updated.

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:16.670
And it will only proceed
to the next batch of three

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:19.520
once the previous three have
passed the health checks.

00:30:22.180 --> 00:30:25.050
Another very common
scenario is canary update.

00:30:25.050 --> 00:30:29.010
So imagine you have a web
store, and you roll out

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:32.460
an update that is supposed
to increase conversion

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:33.207
to purchases.

00:30:33.207 --> 00:30:35.790
Of course, you are not going to
roll out it to the whole group

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:36.480
right away.

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:39.990
You'll probably wants to
canary it, let's say to 30%,

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:41.140
and wait for a while.

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:43.980
Maybe a week, maybe a month,
to collect enough data

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:46.500
to prove that the conversion
is indeed improved.

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:49.070
And roll it forward
if that's the case,

00:30:49.070 --> 00:30:53.055
or roll back if
it didn't happen.

00:30:53.055 --> 00:30:53.930
So let's have a look.

00:30:57.098 --> 00:30:57.890
CEZARY PIEKACZ: OK.

00:30:57.890 --> 00:31:01.890
So let's do both things
at the same time,

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:03.120
both things what Mykola said.

00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:07.100
So let's do a canary update
and a rolling [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:08.690
So we have four VMs.

00:31:08.690 --> 00:31:12.500
All of these are created
from the instance template 1.

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:14.510
Before I will start
the rollout process

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:17.790
I need to tell you a few
words about this application.

00:31:17.790 --> 00:31:20.150
It's a simple
application that monitors

00:31:20.150 --> 00:31:22.220
the internal status of the VM.

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:24.830
So it will show you
when the VM will

00:31:24.830 --> 00:31:28.760
start creating, when it's
going to be deleted, when

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:30.620
it's restarting, recreating.

00:31:30.620 --> 00:31:34.220
And it will also show you
the version of the template

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:36.030
that it's used to create.

00:31:36.030 --> 00:31:39.650
So you can see that we have four
application, four VMs running

00:31:39.650 --> 00:31:43.270
from instance template 1.

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:46.960
So now let's
initiate the update.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:50.220
Let's say we want to have
half of our fleet serving

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:53.770
from new version
of the application.

00:31:53.770 --> 00:32:01.665
And we'll also want to spin
up one new VM at a time.

00:32:01.665 --> 00:32:03.040
So what it means
that we will not

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:05.350
exceed the number
five of VMs that

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:06.690
are running at the same time.

00:32:14.514 --> 00:32:16.470
All right.

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:16.970
OK.

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:22.750
You can see right now that
the new VM is being created.

00:32:22.750 --> 00:32:27.160
It usually takes about 30 to
45 seconds for the process

00:32:27.160 --> 00:32:28.630
to be completed.

00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:30.690
So we need to wait a bit.

00:32:34.100 --> 00:32:40.310
Once this VM is up and running,
and managed instance group

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:44.220
verified that it's
created successfully,

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:48.420
one of the older VMs that
were created using instance

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:50.810
template version
1 will be deleted.

00:32:50.810 --> 00:32:56.600
Once this VM, the deletion
process will be completed,

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:59.930
will spin up additional
VMs, additional one VM.

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:05.497
So you will see another
bar with a green color,

00:33:05.497 --> 00:33:06.580
and the process continues.

00:33:06.580 --> 00:33:10.190
So right now, you can see
that the instance is created.

00:33:10.190 --> 00:33:13.090
It's created from the
version template 2.

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:16.240
So we have a new application,
new version application

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:17.590
running on the VM.

00:33:17.590 --> 00:33:22.840
And once the process is
completed, one of the older VMs

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:24.890
was starting to be deleted.

00:33:24.890 --> 00:33:28.900
And as I said, the process will
continue once half of the fleet

00:33:28.900 --> 00:33:32.705
will be served from the manual
version of the applications.

00:33:32.705 --> 00:33:33.655
Back to you, Mykola.

00:33:35.978 --> 00:33:37.020
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: Yeah.

00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:38.640
And what's great to
note is that when

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:41.730
you do a canary update, let's
say to half of the fleet,

00:33:41.730 --> 00:33:45.030
even if the group is autoscaled,
it will automatically

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:48.210
maintain this ratio for you
if you specify a percentage.

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:50.640
But you can also specify
a fixed number of VMs

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:54.970
and canary one, or two,
or three VMs as you wish.

00:33:54.970 --> 00:33:57.540
So another interesting scenario
that is worth looking at

00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:59.760
is if you have a highly
available workload

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:02.760
that you really don't
want to disrupt at all,

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:07.470
but you want to roll out update
as quickly as possible, you

00:34:07.470 --> 00:34:09.510
can set the new
template to 100%,

00:34:09.510 --> 00:34:12.120
so you want to update
the whole group.

00:34:12.120 --> 00:34:13.980
But you set max
unavailable to zero

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:16.500
so that none of the old
serving VMs is touched,

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:19.449
and instead max surge to 100%.

00:34:19.449 --> 00:34:21.250
So what's going to
happen is that the group

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:25.030
will create 100% more
VMs in the new version,

00:34:25.030 --> 00:34:28.320
wait until those VMs
pass the health checks,

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:30.199
and only then remove
the old version.

00:34:30.199 --> 00:34:33.040
So this way you get
highly available update,

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:37.389
but which is fast
at the same time.

00:34:37.389 --> 00:34:41.010
So how does updater coordinate
with the other automation

00:34:41.010 --> 00:34:42.989
services?

00:34:42.989 --> 00:34:44.850
Let's say updater
and autohealing.

00:34:44.850 --> 00:34:47.520
You have specified that your
disruption budget is two,

00:34:47.520 --> 00:34:50.139
so you can update two
machines at a time,

00:34:50.139 --> 00:34:54.150
take them out of service, and
updater successfully did that.

00:34:54.150 --> 00:34:57.780
Now, as the updater is about
to get to the next batch,

00:34:57.780 --> 00:35:00.230
one of the old machines fails.

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:03.780
Autohealing will detect that
and will start recreating it.

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:06.450
But will updater take
down two more machines,

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:07.920
or one for an update?

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:11.040
The good news is that updater
is aware of the disruption

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:13.260
and will respect your
configuration of max

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:17.280
unavailable 2, and will take
only one extra machines down

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:21.390
for an update so that
your workload is still

00:35:21.390 --> 00:35:24.120
serving at the capacity
that you can tolerate.

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:27.180
And in the end, the one machine
gets updated and another one

00:35:27.180 --> 00:35:29.980
gets autohealed.

00:35:29.980 --> 00:35:32.920
How about updater
and autoscaling?

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:36.820
Let's say updater recreates two
machines from the new instance

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:37.390
template.

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:39.850
And of course, while these
machines are being recreated

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:42.620
they remain in the VM so
we'll get a bit more traffic.

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:46.720
And the average CPU
load will spike.

00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:51.040
Will autoscaler
create extra machines?

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:52.570
The answer is no.

00:35:52.570 --> 00:35:56.200
Autoscaler is aware that the
machines that are being updated

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:57.910
will be back up
and running soon,

00:35:57.910 --> 00:36:00.730
so it will keep the group
size at the same value.

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:05.290
And once the machines are
back, it will be serving again.

00:36:07.860 --> 00:36:11.220
How about updater and regional
managed instance group?

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:15.000
Updater will update the
machines evenly across the zones

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:17.140
to spread the risk.

00:36:17.140 --> 00:36:19.630
And once the new machines
are up and running,

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:22.720
it will also remove
the old machines

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:26.620
evenly across the zones again
to spread the risk for you,

00:36:26.620 --> 00:36:29.110
for high availability.

00:36:29.110 --> 00:36:31.090
Finally, let's have
a look at how updater

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:33.250
works with a load balancer.

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:37.280
So before taking and recreating
a machine for an update,

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:41.300
the machine will first
stop receiving new traffic.

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:45.500
Then it will wait for a
connection draining time out

00:36:45.500 --> 00:36:47.360
to drain the active sessions.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:51.360
You can configure it in your
load balancer configuration.

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:53.080
And only once the
sessions are drained,

00:36:53.080 --> 00:36:56.470
updater will recreate the
instance from the new template,

00:36:56.470 --> 00:37:00.060
wait until it becomes healthy.

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:03.660
And after that, the new machine
will start serving traffic.

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:06.180
So the updater's
really very graceful

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:11.050
and won't interrupt any of
the existing connections.

00:37:11.050 --> 00:37:12.280
So there we have it.

00:37:12.280 --> 00:37:15.040
Managed instance groups allow
you to build highly available,

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:18.790
scalable applications that are
easy to update automatically,

00:37:18.790 --> 00:37:20.770
and will take care of
application failure

00:37:20.770 --> 00:37:23.740
with autohealing, will
protect your application

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:26.350
against zonal failure
by distributing machines

00:37:26.350 --> 00:37:29.620
across multiple zones,
will also tailor group

00:37:29.620 --> 00:37:33.190
size to the variable
load with autoscaling,

00:37:33.190 --> 00:37:37.610
and also protect updates
from unhealthy new versions,

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:42.080
but if the machines are healthy,
roll it out automatically

00:37:42.080 --> 00:37:44.000
so that you can
have peace of mind

00:37:44.000 --> 00:37:46.880
and focus on your
more important tasks

00:37:46.880 --> 00:37:51.330
knowing that your
workload is taken care of.

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:55.410
Now let's have a look at the
rightsizing your machines.

00:37:55.410 --> 00:37:58.170
We've talked about that it's
not very easy to pick up

00:37:58.170 --> 00:37:59.457
the right shape for a machine.

00:37:59.457 --> 00:38:01.290
Of course, the shape
that you have used back

00:38:01.290 --> 00:38:04.110
in your on-prem data
center will work,

00:38:04.110 --> 00:38:08.400
but there are many more
potentially possible

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:10.980
combinations of CPU
cores and memory

00:38:10.980 --> 00:38:12.570
that are available
on Compute Engine.

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:15.540
So how do you know
that you don't overpay?

00:38:15.540 --> 00:38:19.200
The good news is that Compute
Engine observes utilization

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:21.330
of a CPU and memory
in your machines

00:38:21.330 --> 00:38:23.730
over the eight
days period, which

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:26.670
is good if your workload
has a weekly load patterns

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:30.090
or more or less persistent
or load over time.

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:31.783
And will recommend
the optimal size

00:38:31.783 --> 00:38:34.200
for your machine if it thinks
that the current size is not

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:36.900
optimal, either for
individual machines

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:40.880
or for VMs in the managed
instance groups that are not

00:38:40.880 --> 00:38:43.080
autoscaled horizontally.

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:45.540
And recommendation can
be either save money

00:38:45.540 --> 00:38:48.780
if your machine is too
big, or, if it consistently

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:52.680
sees that the utilization,
for example, is very high,

00:38:52.680 --> 00:38:55.230
it might suggest you to get
a bigger machine to improve

00:38:55.230 --> 00:38:58.080
performance of your workload.

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:03.540
And what's great is that we have
the Recommendations API already

00:39:03.540 --> 00:39:06.270
available in Alpha,
which allows you

00:39:06.270 --> 00:39:08.640
to get all of the
recommendations

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:11.520
from your projects
programmatically via RESTful

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:12.870
API.

00:39:12.870 --> 00:39:16.170
And then with the data,
you can aggregate, triage,

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:18.210
prioritize the
recommendation as it

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:20.220
makes sense for
your organization,

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.530
build dashboard
supports for your teams,

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:27.840
visualize savings, and
whatnot to help you manage

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:31.260
the costs of your deployments.

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:34.170
And one more pro
tip is that if you

00:39:34.170 --> 00:39:36.030
want more accurate
recommendations,

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:39.600
use Stackdriver monitoring
agent on your machines,

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:42.930
as it has a better
visibility into utilization

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:43.960
inside the machine.

00:39:43.960 --> 00:39:45.630
And as a result will
increase quality

00:39:45.630 --> 00:39:48.055
of the recommendations for you.

00:39:48.055 --> 00:39:48.930
So let's have a look.

00:39:52.423 --> 00:39:53.920
CEZARY PIEKACZ: All right.

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:57.340
We have five
different instances.

00:39:57.340 --> 00:40:01.320
So let's imagine that you
have a virtual machine

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:04.620
on your own data
center, and you move it

00:40:04.620 --> 00:40:11.170
to the GCP, the Google Cloud,
using the exact same size.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:16.260
So for example, we have a
machine that has four CPU cores

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.120
and 50 gigabytes of memory.

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:21.330
But what happens if your
application does not

00:40:21.330 --> 00:40:25.710
use this CPU power and memory?

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:28.020
We'll detect it,
and after one day,

00:40:28.020 --> 00:40:30.690
we'll give you the
recommendation to size

00:40:30.690 --> 00:40:34.120
it down to save some money.

00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:37.450
So resizing the
VM is very simple.

00:40:37.450 --> 00:40:39.300
You just click on the Resize.

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:42.190
You can customize the VM size.

00:40:42.190 --> 00:40:47.100
For example, if you know that
your application will not even

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:50.100
use two CPUs as we
recommended, you

00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:55.790
can even scale it down to
one CPU, and you click Apply.

00:40:55.790 --> 00:41:00.680
So now what happens is we will
automatically stop the VM,

00:41:00.680 --> 00:41:04.100
change the VM's
shape, and start it up

00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:08.278
without you having
to do anything else.

00:41:08.278 --> 00:41:10.070
But this is the one
type of recommendation.

00:41:10.070 --> 00:41:13.910
So it's basically telling you,
size down to save some money.

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:18.850
But what if you've created a VM,
you've started the application,

00:41:18.850 --> 00:41:22.930
and right now the application
is very high-CPU intensive?

00:41:22.930 --> 00:41:25.090
For example you have this
web server application

00:41:25.090 --> 00:41:27.460
that is doing some,
or workloads that

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:29.170
are doing the computations.

00:41:29.170 --> 00:41:31.000
Again, we detected it.

00:41:31.000 --> 00:41:32.500
We can verify that.

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:35.590
There is a process that
it's utilizing one hundredth

00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:37.670
of the CPU all the time.

00:41:37.670 --> 00:41:38.800
So we detected it.

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:41.320
And we are saying,
basically, your instance

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:47.290
is under-utilized, and
you should scale it up.

00:41:47.290 --> 00:41:49.960
And you can apply the
same recommendations

00:41:49.960 --> 00:41:51.190
in the same way.

00:41:51.190 --> 00:41:57.730
So click Resize, change
the number of CPUs or RAM,

00:41:57.730 --> 00:41:58.810
and be good.

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:02.890
The same thing who works
for managed instance groups.

00:42:02.890 --> 00:42:06.610
So you can see that right now,
we have a managed instance

00:42:06.610 --> 00:42:09.130
group with two instances.

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:12.415
And again, they have a
low memory usage, low CPU

00:42:12.415 --> 00:42:13.000
utilization.

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:18.400
So you can change the instance
template to the smaller one

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:20.720
and save some money each month.

00:42:23.450 --> 00:42:23.950
OK.

00:42:23.950 --> 00:42:30.020
So I will now show you how
to quickly use the API.

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:35.740
So this is a simple one-line
code using [INAUDIBLE]

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:38.980
that will just query
the recommendations,

00:42:38.980 --> 00:42:46.600
and will basically get the
same information that you've

00:42:46.600 --> 00:42:49.210
seen on the screen.

00:42:49.210 --> 00:42:55.330
So for example here, we can see
some information about the zone

00:42:55.330 --> 00:43:01.240
and that we will save $74 US if
we apply these recommendations.

00:43:01.240 --> 00:43:03.790
And using it, you can
build, as Mykola said,

00:43:03.790 --> 00:43:06.790
a simple dashboard that will
aggregate all information

00:43:06.790 --> 00:43:08.440
from all the VMs
in your projects

00:43:08.440 --> 00:43:13.330
from all the projects
that are created by you.

00:43:13.330 --> 00:43:19.420
For example, the sum and give
you the total savings per month

00:43:19.420 --> 00:43:23.660
from all the recommendations
that you saw.

00:43:23.660 --> 00:43:26.470
Can you get back to
the slides, please?

00:43:26.470 --> 00:43:27.720
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: All right.

00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:29.760
So in short, rightsizing
recommendations

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:33.150
help you to save costs
on your deployments,

00:43:33.150 --> 00:43:38.400
improve performance, and
what's most important,

00:43:38.400 --> 00:43:40.718
relieve you of the pain to
make the decision of what

00:43:40.718 --> 00:43:41.760
machine size do you have.

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:43.300
There is no wrong decision.

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:45.840
Even if the machine
size is not optimal,

00:43:45.840 --> 00:43:49.230
recommendations will
detect that based

00:43:49.230 --> 00:43:52.860
on the utilization that
they observe and suggest you

00:43:52.860 --> 00:43:57.240
a size that it thinks
might be a better fit.

00:43:57.240 --> 00:43:59.950
So bring it all together.

00:43:59.950 --> 00:44:02.910
We have looked at how
to deploy application

00:44:02.910 --> 00:44:06.810
with the containers on
managed instance groups.

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:12.750
We've built highly available and
scalable stateless applications

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:15.580
on top of managed
instance groups.

00:44:15.580 --> 00:44:18.040
And we have also
looked at how to apply

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:20.350
rightsizing recommendations
to your VMs.

00:44:20.350 --> 00:44:22.300
And I want to leave
you with this,

00:44:22.300 --> 00:44:25.840
that even if you have
to deploy your workloads

00:44:25.840 --> 00:44:28.210
to virtual machines
to have full control

00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:31.960
over your infrastructure, you
don't have to manage everything

00:44:31.960 --> 00:44:32.960
by hand.

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:39.070
You can have highly scalable
and available deployments

00:44:39.070 --> 00:44:41.800
by picking and choosing
recommendations,

00:44:41.800 --> 00:44:44.300
as well as automated
services, such as autoscaling,

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:47.590
autohealing, autoupdating,
that work for you.

00:44:47.590 --> 00:44:49.670
You are not on your own.

00:44:49.670 --> 00:44:50.740
Thank you.

